
SCIENTIFIC WHITE SHEET v2.0
Multi‑Agent Lunar Civilizational Engine — Distributed Bio‑Mechanical Growth Architecture (D‑BMGA)
Author: C. Matthew Blackwell
Phase: Multi‑Agent Lunar Deployment
Status: Fully Autonomous, Closed‑Loop, Self‑Expanding Infrastructure Species

1. System Identity v2.0
The D‑BMGA v2.0 is a distributed, multi‑agent, dual‑material growth organism capable of:
autonomous subterranean construction
metabolic extraction of lunar regolith
multi‑agent coordination across city‑scale networks
adaptive response to lunar seismic, thermal, and vacuum physics
long‑term self‑healing and structural optimization
It behaves as a civilizational substrate, not a machine.

2. Governing Fields v2.0
2.1 Rigid Density Field ( \rho_r )
Load‑bearing geopolymer lattice; thickens under compression and thermal cycling.
2.2 Flexible Density Field ( \rho_f )
Viscoelastic tendon sheath; regulates pressure, vibration, and thermal expansion.
2.3 Damage Field ( D )
Tracks microfractures, thermal fatigue, and impact shock.
2.4 Signal Potential Field ( \phi )
Routes microfluidic agents for healing, oxygen routing, and resource distribution.
2.5 Vibration Field ( v )
Encodes lunar seismic scattering (long‑duration, low‑attenuation).
2.6 Soil‑Interface Gradient ( G_s )
Basaltic strata, glassy regolith, void pockets, and lava‑tube curvature.
2.7 Metabolic Resource Field ( M )
Tracks oxide content, anorthosite availability, and regolith feedstock.

3. Evolution Equations v2.0
3.1 Multi‑Agent Deposition
[ \frac{\partial \rho_r}{\partial t} = \sum_i k_{r,i} \sigma_{c,i}^+ - \lambda_r D ]
3.2 Metabolic Extraction
[ \frac{\partial M}{\partial t} = -\eta , \rho_{tip} + \delta , \phi ]
3.3 Collaborative Healing
[ \frac{\partial D}{\partial t} = \alpha \sigma_{eq} - \beta \sum_i \phi_i ]
3.4 Multi‑Agent Hazard Potential
[ H = w_1 G_s + w_2 v + w_3 D + w_4 (1 - M) ]
3.5 Swarm Tip Motion
[ \mathbf{v}{tip,i} = -\nabla H + \sum{j \neq i} \Gamma_{ij} ]
where ( \Gamma_{ij} ) encodes cooperative merging and collision avoidance.

4. Swarm Intelligence v2.0
4.1 Predictive Look‑Ahead
Each growth tip samples hazard fields 3–7 radii ahead.
4.2 Cooperative Merging
Agents fuse tunnels when metabolic and structural gradients align.
4.3 Resource Arbitration
Microfluidic nodes negotiate regolith flow, oxygen routing, and binder synthesis.
4.4 City‑Scale Coordination
Distributed memory ensures no two agents over‑mine or over‑reinforce the same region.

5. Metabolic Budgets v2.0
5.1 Regolith Ingestion
Growth tips swallow raw lunar dust; particle size distribution affects deposition rate.
5.2 Ilmenite Cracking
[ \text{FeTiO}_3 \rightarrow \text{FeO} + \text{TiO}_2 + \text{O}_2 ]
5.3 Oxygen Routing
O₂ is stored in tendon‑based pressure chambers or vented into hollow voids.
5.4 Geopolymer Synthesis
Silicate‑rich regolith is converted into rigid lattice material.
5.5 Thermal Budget
Night‑cycle freezing strengthens rigid cores; day‑cycle heating relaxes tendon skins.

